# Messages [17 messages]: [{'role': 'user', 'content': "System prompt: You are a helpful assistant. I will now give you a documentand please answer my question afterwards based on the content in document###Crux: GPU-Efficient Communication Scheduling for Deep Learning Training  \nAuthors: Jiamin Cao, Yu Guan, Kun Qian, Jiaqi Gao, Wencong Xiao, Jianbo Dong, Binzhang Fu, Dennis Cai, Ennan Zhai (Alibaba Cloud)\n\nAbstract  \nCrux is a communication scheduler that addresses inter-job communication contention in deep learning training (DLT) clusters, improving GPU utilization. By defining GPU intensity and tailoring scheduling accordingly, Crux increases GPU utilization up to 23% over state-of-the-art approaches.\n\n1. Introduction  \nModern GPU clusters face GPU underutilization due to communication contention among co-executing DLT jobs. Crux introduces GPU intensity as a prioritization metric and shows that current schedulers don't adequately handle inter-job contention.\n\n2. Background and Motivation  \nProfiling a 2,000+ GPU production cluster shows that 36.3% of jobs face contention. Inter-job communication contention significantly affects GPU utilization. The paper argues that optimizing GPU utilization—not just JCT—is critical for cost and efficiency.\n\n3. Methodology and System Overview  \nMaximizing GPU utilization is modeled as a flow scheduling problem using GPU intensity (computation/communication ratio). The system overview includes GPU intensity-based path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression.\n\n4. Crux Design  \n- 4.1 Path Selection: Avoids congestion for high-intensity jobs.  \n- 4.2 Priority Assignment: Corrects GPU intensity based on job characteristics (e.g., iteration time, overlap).  \n- 4.3 Priority Compression: Uses DAG-based Max-K-Cut to map many jobs to limited NIC priority levels.  \n- 4.4 Validation: Simulations show Crux achieves over 97% optimal performance for each scheduling step.\n\n5. Implementation  \nCrux is implemented in 7K LOC and integrates with PyTorch, TensorFlow, and X-DeepLearning. Crux Daemon and Crux Transport modules handle job profiling, probing network paths, and enforcing decisions via RDMA APIs.\n\n6. Evaluation  \n- 6.1 Testbed: 96-GPU testbed shows up to 14.8% GPU utilization gain and 33% JCT reduction.  \n- 6.2 Trace Simulation: On a 2,000+ GPU cluster trace, Crux outperforms Sincronia, TACCL, and CASSINI by 4–23%.  \n- 6.3 Scheduler Compatibility: Crux boosts performance even when combined with HiveD or Muri.  \n- 6.4 GPU Intensity Visualization: Darker flow patterns correlate with higher GPU utilization under Crux.\n\n7. Discussions  \n- 7.1 Limitations: Crux simplifies complex overlap patterns; real-world variance may cause suboptimal prioritization.  \n- 7.2 Fairness: Higher priority jobs may slightly degrade others; fairness extensions possible.  \n- 7.3 Topology Adaptability: Crux’s design generalizes across Clos, double-sided, and potentially other topologies.\n\n8. Related Work  \nCrux differs from job-level schedulers and prior intra-job communication solutions. It also surpasses inter-job tools like CASSINI by using real-time GPU intensity metrics and adaptive scheduling.\n\n9. Conclusion  \nCrux introduces GPU intensity as a foundational metric for DLT communication scheduling. Through path selection, priority assignment, and priority compression, it significantly improves cluster GPU utilization.\n\nAppendices  \n- A: Proof that GPU intensity-based flow scheduling correlates with utilization.  \n- B: Theoretical grounding for DAG-based Max-K-Cut in priority compression."}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': 'Got it!'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'What is the primary goal of Crux in deep learning training clusters?'}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': ' goal goal of Crux is to to to to improve. the scheduling in deep. training) clusters and clusters.,,, the..'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'Which metric does Crux introduce to prioritize communicat